-- Pong — the fixture the prototype exists to run.
--
-- Nothing here names a console. Two unit systems do the work:
--
--   * absolute — `1 cell` is one 8x8 hardware cell. Use it where a thing is
--     what it is everywhere: a one-tile ball, a one-tile-tall paddle, a score
--     in the corner.
--   * relative — `15vw` is 15% of the playfield's width, `40vmin` is 40% of its
--     shorter side. Use it for anything that has to stay *balanced*: a paddle
--     must cover the same fraction of the wall, and a rally must take the same
--     number of seconds, whether the court is 20 cells wide or 40.
--
-- Sizes quantise to whole cells; speeds and positions do not.

loop title

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- title -----

scene title

create text prompt in title (x centerx - 7, y centery, text "press a to play")

when a pressed in title (scene) as play

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------- play -----

scene play

-- The ball is one cell on every console — it is the smallest thing that can be
-- drawn — but it crosses the court in the same 2.5 seconds everywhere.
create object ball (width 1 cell, height 1 cell, speed 40vmin, sprite ball.svg)

-- The paddle covers 15% of the wall and can traverse the court in 1.7 seconds,
-- on a Game Boy and on a Mega Drive alike.
create object paddle (width 15vw, height 1 cell, speed 60vw, sprite paddle.svg)

create ball ball1 in play (x centerx, y centery, direction southwest)

create paddle paddle1 in play (x centerx, y screenheight - 1)

-- The opponent is a touch slower than the player, which is the whole of its
-- handicap. Everything else about it is the same object.
create paddle paddle2 in play (x centerx, y 0, speed 45vw)

create number score1 in play (value 0, x 1, y 1)
create number score2 in play (value 0, x screenwidth - 2, y 1)

-- The player drives the bottom paddle. `on hold` restores the previous value
-- when the button comes up, so releasing left leaves the paddle stopped.
control paddle1 left (xdirection -1) on hold
control paddle1 right (xdirection 1) on hold

-- The opponent is one level-triggered rule, not a special "ai" feature.
--
-- It is *proportional*, not on/off: `xdirection` is how far away the ball is,
-- measured in ticks of the paddle's own travel, clamped to full speed. Far away
-- it runs flat out; as it arrives the number shrinks and it eases in and stops
-- exactly on target. An on/off version overshoots by a tick every tick and
-- either buzzes or, with a dead zone wide enough to stop that, moves in visible
-- blocks. This does neither.
--
-- `1.5vw` is the gain: with the paddle at 45vw per second and 60 ticks per
-- second it covers 0.75vw per tick, so the ball is at full chase just over two
-- ticks out. Both numbers are relative, so the feel ports unchanged.
when always in play (paddle2.xdirection) as clamp((ball1.centerx - paddle2.centerx) / 1.5vw, -1, 1)

-- Walls.
when ball hits screenleft, screenright (xdirection) as flip
when paddle hits screenleft, screenright (xdirection) as 0

-- The bounce. `ball` and `paddle` bind to whichever two objects collided, and
-- the horizontal component comes out of where on the paddle the ball landed.
when ball hits paddle (ydirection, xdirection) as (flip, (ball.centerx - paddle.centerx) / paddle.width)

-- Scoring. A miss resets the ball to the middle, serving toward whoever
-- conceded, and every value in one rule is computed before any of them lands.
when ball hits screentop (score1.value, ball1.x, ball1.y, ball1.ydirection) as (score1.value + 1, centerx, centery, 1)
when ball hits screenbottom (score2.value, ball1.x, ball1.y, ball1.ydirection) as (score2.value + 1, centerx, centery, -1)

when score1.value reaches 10 in play (scene) as gameover
when score2.value reaches 10 in play (scene) as gameover

-- ------------------------------------------------------------- game over ----

scene gameover

create text result in gameover (x centerx - 4, y centery, text "game over")

when a pressed in gameover (scene) as play
